data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"The internet","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" is often described as a \"cloud,\" but in reality, it is a massive","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":", tangible infrastructure of wires, glass, and specialized computers. To understand how it works, we must trace the journey of","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" data from your device to a server and back again, moving from the physical layer to the logical protocols that govern communication.\n\n### The","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Physical Foundation: Cables and Signals\nAt the lowest level, the internet is a global network of physical cables. Under the oceans","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" lie massive fiber-optic cables that connect continents, while underground conduits link cities and neighborhoods. These fiber-optic cables transmit","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" data as pulses of light, which can travel at incredible speeds over vast distances.\n\nWhen you connect to the internet via","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Wi-Fi, your device sends radio waves to a router. The router then converts those wireless signals into electrical or optical","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" signals that travel through your local ISP (Internet Service Provider) infrastructure. This physical layer is the \"highway\" upon which all","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" digital information travels.\n\n### Addressing: IP and DNS\nTo send data across this highway, every device needs an address. This","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" is the **Internet Protocol (IP) address**, a unique string of numbers (e.g., 192.1","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"68.1.1) assigned to every device connected to the network. \n\nHowever, humans cannot memorize strings","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" of numbers for every website. This is where the **Domain Name System (DNS)** comes in. Think of DNS as the","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" internet’s phonebook. When you type \"google.com\" into your browser, your computer sends a request to a DNS","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" server, which looks up the IP address associated with that domain name and returns it to your browser. Now that your computer knows the","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" destination IP, it can begin the conversation.\n\n### The Language of Data: TCP/IP\nData is not sent as one","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" giant file. Instead, it is broken down into small, manageable chunks called **packets**. This process is governed by the **Transmission Control Protocol","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" (TCP)**. \n\nTCP ensures that data is reliable. It numbers each packet so that if they arrive out of order,","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the receiving computer can reassemble them correctly. If a packet is lost during transmission, TCP detects the gap and requests that","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the sender retransmit the missing piece. This ensures that the file you receive—whether it’s an image, a video","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":", or a webpage—is complete and error-free.\n\n### Routing: Finding the Path\nOnce your packets are ready","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":", they enter the network. They don't take a single, fixed path. Instead, they travel through a series of **routers","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"**. Routers are the traffic cops of the internet; they examine the destination IP address on each packet and decide the most","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" efficient path to the next \"hop\" in the network. \n\nBecause the internet is a mesh network, if one cable","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" is cut or a router is overloaded, the packets can dynamically reroute through a different path. This decentralization is what makes the internet so resilient","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":".\n\n### The Application Layer: HTTP/HTTPS\nOnce the packets arrive at the destination server, the server processes the request","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":". This is where **HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol)** or its secure version, **HTTPS**, comes into play. \n\nHTTP is","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the language of the web. When your browser requests a webpage, it sends an HTTP \"GET\" request. The server responds","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" with the content of the page—usually in the form of HTML (Hypertext Markup Language), CSS (for styling), and JavaScript","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" (for interactivity). \n\nHTTPS adds a layer of security using **TLS (Transport Layer Security)**. Before the actual data is exchanged, your","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" browser and the server perform a \"handshake,\" agreeing on encryption keys. This ensures that even if someone intercepts the packets while they are traveling","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" across the physical cables, they cannot read the contents.\n\n### Putting It All Together\nTo visualize the entire process:\n1. **Request","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":":** You type a URL. Your browser asks a DNS server for the IP address.\n2. **Packaging:** Your browser creates","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" an HTTP request, which is wrapped in TCP packets.\n3. **Transmission:** The packets are sent to your router, converted","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" into light or electrical signals, and sent through the ISP’s network.\n4. **Routing:** Routers across","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the globe pass the packets along until they reach the destination server.\n5. **Response:** The server receives the packets, re","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"assembles them, processes the HTTP request, and sends the webpage data back to you in the same manner.\n6. **Rendering:**","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Your browser receives the packets, reassembles the HTML, and renders the page on your screen.\n\nThis entire process,","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" which involves traveling thousands of miles and passing through dozens of routers, often happens in a fraction of a second. The internet is a marvel","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" of engineering—a complex, layered system that manages to turn light pulses and electrical currents into the vast, interconnected digital world","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" we use every day.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

